      <!-- ============ Unit Economics & Capacity ============ -->
      <section id="economics" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">05 · Unit Economics & Capacity</span>
        <h2>Transactions cannot replace physical capacity.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The model is deliberately transaction-driven rather than attendance-driven — but
          parking, dwell time, party size, conversion, production, checkout, restrooms, and
          circulation collectively determine how many transactions the property can physically
          support. This section states the capacity math, shows the daily bridge behind the
          55,000-transaction <em>management target</em>, marks 40–45k as the underwriting case,
          and defines the dashboard that will replace every assumption with pilot measurements.
        </p>

        <h3>One vocabulary, used everywhere</h3>
        <p>
          Every volume figure in this plan uses the following definitions. Conversion is always
          measured on parties — a family of five that buys one dozen is one converted party, one
          food order, and five guest visits.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {unitTerms.map((t) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{t.term}</strong></td><td>{t.def}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>The capacity equation</h3>
        <p>
          Daily on-site volume follows one chain, applied below at a lower, base, and upper
          operating case — a <em>capacity range, not a demand forecast</em>.
        </p>
        <div class="eq-block" role="img" aria-label="Capacity chain: parking spaces times open hours divided by average dwell times occupied utilization equals arriving parties; arriving parties times guests per vehicle equals guest visits; arriving parties times food conversion, plus express orders, equals food orders; food orders times average order equals food revenue.">
          <span>parking spaces × (open hours ÷ avg. dwell) × utilization = <strong>arriving parties</strong></span>
          <span>arriving parties × guests per vehicle = <strong>guest visits</strong></span>
          <span>arriving parties × food conversion + express orders = <strong>food orders</strong></span>
          <span>food orders × average order = <strong>food revenue</strong></span>
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Capacity input</th><th class="num">Lower case</th><th class="num">Illustrative base</th><th class="num">Upper operating case</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {capacityInputs.map((r) => (
                <tr><td>{r.input}</td><td class="num">{r.low}</td><td class="num">{r.base}</td><td class="num">{r.high}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="figure-note">
          Average dwell runs opposite the other inputs by design: the lower case assumes guests
          linger longest (2.25 hr), which slows parking turnover and reduces daily capacity;
          the upper case assumes the quickest visits (1.50 hr).
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Resulting daily capacity</th><th class="num">Lower case</th><th class="num">Illustrative base</th><th class="num">Upper operating case</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {capacityOutputs.map((r) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{r.output}</strong></td><td class="num">{r.low}</td><td class="num">{r.base}</td><td class="num">{r.high}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The base case works through as ≈525 arriving parties, ≈1,416 guest visits, ≈341 food
          orders, and ≈$6,991 of food revenue — about <strong>$7.61 of food revenue per occupied
          parking-space-hour</strong> at a $20.50 average ticket, the single number that
          integrates dwell, conversion, and basket size. The upper case is a very busy day: at
          more than 2,600 guest visits, ingress, restrooms, order capture, and circulation must
          all still function, and the property would not feel “small-scale” even though the
          building is modest.
        </p>

        <h3>The daily bridge behind 55,000 transactions</h3>
        <p>
          The Year-One food line is not one number; it is a day-type model across roughly 195
          public operating days (April–December, with reduced early-week schedules outside
          peak). Characterize the ladder honestly:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Food transactions</th><th>Characterization</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>40–45k</strong></td><td>Defensible underwriting / lender case</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>55k</strong></td><td>Successful Year-1 management case — still the operating goal</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>70k</strong></td><td>Strong early-scale operation</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>~85k</strong></td><td>Mature stabilization with $550–650k+ EBITDA target</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Fifty-five thousand remains the Year-1 operating goal; financing does not require
          hitting it in Year 1. Each additional $1 in average F&amp;B ticket at ~85k transactions
          materially improves contribution without more parking, staffing, or customer
          acquisition — which is why the ticket target moved from $17.50 to $20–21.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Operating-day type</th><th class="num">Days</th><th class="num">Avg. food transactions</th><th class="num">Annual transactions</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {dayTypes.map((d) => (
                <tr><td>{d.type}</td><td class="num">{d.days}</td><td class="num">{d.avg}</td><td class="num">{d.annual}</td></tr>
              ))}
              <tr class="total"><td>Total</td><td class="num">195</td><td class="num"></td><td class="num">≈55,050</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          At a ≈$20.50 average, the management target produces the ≈$1.13M Year-One
          food-and-beverage line. The implied physical load is disclosed rather than hidden:
          ≈76,400 visiting parties and on the order of 206,000 annual guest visits at 2.7 guests
          per vehicle — roughly 1,050 people per operating day. Peak dependence remains explicit:
          the 30 peak days produce 19,500 of the 55,000 orders — <strong>35% of annual food
          transactions on 15% of operating days</strong>. Bulk/off-site and retreat revenue
          reduce pure dependence on those days; opening liquidity is still sized for weather loss.
        </p>

        <details class="case-study">
          <summary>
            <span class="case-flag">Stress vignette · Optional reading</span>
            <span class="case-title">Five bad peak days remain a material stress — even with a stronger ticket</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="case-body">
            <p>
              Seasonal businesses live with peak concentration. On the 55k management path, five
              lost 650-order days are not an exotic tail risk — they are a plausible bad autumn.
            </p>
            <div class="eq-block" role="img" aria-label="3,250 lost transactions times 20.50 dollars equals about 66,600 dollars of food and beverage sales; at 67.6 percent gross margin that is about 45,000 dollars of food and beverage gross margin.">
              <span>5 days × 650 orders = <strong>3,250 lost transactions</strong></span>
              <span>3,250 × $20.50 ≈ <strong>$66,625 F&amp;B sales</strong></span>
              <span>× ≈67.6% F&amp;B gross margin ≈ <strong>$45,000 F&amp;B GM</strong> before other spend</span>
            </div>
            <p>
              The model answers with accessible opening liquidity of $450–500K,
              growing bulk/off-site and retreat contribution outside peak weekends, and a
              downside case that must remain solvent after a weak autumn — not by pretending
              October never rains.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <h3>Why express channels matter more than preorders</h3>
        <p>
          A 650-transaction peak day exposes the constraint:
        </p>
        <div class="eq-block" role="img" aria-label="650 orders at 65 percent conversion imply about 1,000 arriving parties; 1,000 parties at a 1.75-hour dwell demand about 1,750 occupied parking-space-hours; but 180 spaces over 8.5 hours at 85 percent utilization supply only about 1,300.">
          <span>650 orders ÷ 65% conversion ≈ <strong>1,000 arriving parties</strong></span>
          <span>1,000 parties × 1.75-hour dwell ≈ <strong>1,750 parking-space-hours demanded</strong></span>
          <span>180 spaces × 8.5 hours × 85% utilization ≈ <strong>1,300 supplied</strong></span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Roughly a quarter of peak transactions must therefore arrive through genuinely
          low-dwell channels. On 30 peak days that is ≈4,875 low-dwell peak transactions —
          more than the annual express drive-up/office/school/bulk budget (~4,500) alone. So
          either a substantial share of the ≈6,400 mobile orders must be true
          <strong>preorder + leave</strong> visits, or the peak-day parking model does not
          close. The plan therefore splits preorder reporting:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Kitchen effect</th><th>Parking effect</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Preorder + stay</strong></td><td>Smooths production</td><td>None — normal dwell</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Preorder + leave</strong></td><td>Smooths production</td><td>Creates capacity</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Express / bulk / drive-up</strong></td><td>Batchable</td><td>Creates capacity</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          Order-ahead smooths kitchen demand. Only preorder-and-leave and true express channels
          create parking capacity.
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Labor productivity: lean opening, then scale</h3>
        <p>
          Payroll is built from the operating calendar, not held flat or selected as a target
          percentage. Public days carry a base schedule; retreats, school days, camps, and
          private programs add only the hours they require. The illustrative Year-1 build is:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Day / use</th><th class="num">Occurrences</th><th class="num">Staff-hours each</th><th class="num">Annual hours</th><th>Productivity check</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {laborBuild.map((r) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{r.use}</strong></td><td class="num">{r.occurrences}</td><td class="num">{r.hours}</td><td class="num">{r.annual}</td><td>{r.productivity}</td></tr>
              ))}
              <tr class="total"><td>Total scheduled hourly labor</td><td></td><td></td><td class="num">15,180</td><td>Before contingency</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Approximately 15,180 scheduled hourly staff-hours at an illustrative fully loaded
          ~$20–21 per hour produce about $304–319K of hourly labor. Approximately $240K for three
          core salaried/regular leadership roles brings Year-1 payroll to about
          <strong>$544–559K</strong>, inside the $525–575K planning range. Retreats are attractive
          in this model because a typical $3–5K booking adds roughly 16 staff-hours, not a
          parallel event-production team.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pilots replace every schedule and productivity assumption with observed staff-hours.
          Year 2 and mature payroll then scale from the measured calendar toward
          <strong>$600–650K and $700–725K</strong>, respectively; those later figures are
          ceilings to rebuild, not automatic annual budgets.
        </p>

        <h3>Capital intensity, defined with denominators</h3>
        <p>
          “Lower capital than attraction and event competitors” needs a denominator — and it
          must include property acquisition. The planning case is roughly
          <strong>$2.5–3.0M all-in</strong> for adaptive reuse (acquisition + renovation).
          New-build paths that recreate ≈$4M+ fragility fail the capital gate. These are the ratios the plan
          tracks, to be replaced with contractor- and site-supported figures before financing:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Ratio</th><th class="num">Plan</th><th class="num">Arithmetic</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {capitalRatios.map((r) => (
                <tr><td>{r.ratio}</td><td class="num"><strong>{r.value}</strong></td><td class="num">{r.note}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>The eight-metric operating dashboard</h3>
        <p>
          Each metric carries a precise denominator, gets a baseline from the pilot seasons, and
          is reported separately for autumn peaks, ordinary public days, and programs. Peak
          handoff time, order accuracy, abandonment, stockouts, and parking queue time remain
          service-level measures beneath these — with handoff and throughput judged at the
          ninetieth percentile, not the average, because averages conceal exactly the periods
          when crowding damages the brand.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Definition</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {dashboard.map((m) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{m.metric}</strong></td><td>{m.def}</td><td>{m.why}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The dashboard is connected, not a list:
        </p>
        <div class="eq-block" role="img" aria-label="Repeat households times visits per household times conversion times average transaction equals core food revenue; orders divided by direct labor hours equals labor productivity; revenue divided by occupied parking-space-hours equals site productivity.">
          <span>repeat households × visits per household × conversion × avg. transaction = <strong>core food revenue</strong></span>
          <span>orders ÷ direct labor hours = <strong>labor productivity</strong></span>
          <span>revenue ÷ occupied parking-space-hours = <strong>site productivity</strong></span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Underperformance can then be diagnosed — insufficient household reach, weak return
          frequency, low conversion, small baskets, excessive dwell, or labor inefficiency —
          rather than debated. Net Promoter Score is treated as a diagnostic alongside observed
          return behavior, never as the primary proof of loyalty. This dashboard is the
          instrument panel of the operating philosophy: it is how each season’s operating
          experience becomes the measurable input for the next.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Financials ============ -->
      <section id="financials" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">06 · Revenue Streams & Financial Plan</span>
        <h2>Free to enter. Worth paying for.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Admission is always free — grounds, market, café, and pumpkin selection, every day,
          including peak autumn weekends. There is no gate fee, no arrival reservation, and no
          upsell ladder. Margin lives in hero products bought many ways — cider, donuts, a
          short coffee menu, bundles, and order-ahead — plus retail and genuinely bounded
          programs: ticketed evenings, workshops, camps, field trips, corporate retreats, and
          standardized private gatherings — plus a distinct bulk/off-site food channel.
        </p>
        <p>
          A $2 Morning Cup and a bag of donuts is a complete, welcome visit — the model treats
          that family as the beginning of a relationship that later books a camp, a field trip,
          or a corporate retreat, not as a missed ticket sale. Basket composition (bundles,
          beverage attachment, take-home, preorder) is how the average moves toward $20–21
          without aggressive menu-price hikes.
        </p>

        <h3>Multiple engines on the same assets</h3>
        <p>
          Food and beverage is about two-thirds of Year-One management-target revenue
          (~$1.13M of ~$1.71M); camps, education, retreats, standardized bookings, and opportunistic
          retail supply the rest. Year-1 F&amp;B gross profit (~$763K) still sits below the full
          operating-cost block — food alone does not carry the year. Mature economics depend
          on the same physical plant earning across seasons:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Economic layer</th><th>Strategic role</th><th>Underwriting treatment</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
