              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          A distinctive place, a narrow set of excellent products, disciplined programming, and
          repeated use of the same assets across different customers and seasons.
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Hero products, sold many ways</h3>
        <p>
          The autumn menu is a production system of roughly a dozen purchase buttons: donuts
          (single, half-dozen, dozen), cider (cup, half-gallon, gallon), a $2 Morning Cup of
          locally roasted batch coffee, a double espresso, an Americano, hot chocolate, a $2
          dog donut, family bundles, and one rotating item — no milk-steaming matrix, syrup
          ladder, or size tree. A staff-operated superautomatic espresso machine keeps the
          coffee automated behind the counter while the service stays human in front of it.
          This is not competing with bakeries: a few singular products, made consistently, with
          secondary pastries curated from partners. Item names carry the same discipline as the
          item count: <strong>“Morning Cup — $2,”</strong> not “$2 coffee”; <strong>“Fresh
          Donut,”</strong> not “Cinnamon Sugar Ring.” Plain, confident names remove decision
          fatigue and read as intentional rather than cheap.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Ordering lane</th><th>Best use</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {lanes.map((l) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{l.lane}</strong></td><td>{l.use}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Order-ahead runs on a managed commerce platform (Square, Toast, or Lightspeed class)
          rather than custom software, on one price list everywhere — walk-up, mobile, and
          drive-up — so no one shops for a better rate while standing in line. Every order is
          handed over at a dedicated staffed pickup counter, physically separate from the
          walk-up queue: no lockers, no customer-facing kiosks. The guest’s incentive is the
          workflow — a kitchen pickup window and food ready on arrival; the kitchen’s payoff is
          batching to known demand, with preorders targeted at 15–25% of peak sales. Pickup
          windows belong to the kitchen, not to admission: they organize handoff timing without
          reserving a place on the grounds. Office and school bulk orders follow the Yates
          mechanic — minimum quantities, 48-hour lead time, volume pricing.
        </p>

        <h3>Clear, confident pricing</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Morning Cup — $2</strong> is a simple, welcoming reason to stop, not a
          cornerstone of the underwriting. The pilots will determine whether its 2030 price
          should be $2, $2.50, or $3.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hero-product ladder does more work: a $3 Fresh Donut, modest half-dozen savings,
          and meaningful dozen savings encourage a box without complicated choices. Leaf &amp;
          Lantern is not a value brand; it is a place where the menu is short, prices are clear,
          and every purchase should feel easy to justify.
        </p>

