          roughly ~$150K of F&amp;B contribution at the higher ticket and must be absorbed without
          forcing permanent-site leverage.
        </p>

        <h3>Startup-cost planning allowance <small>(adaptive reuse)</small></h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th class="num">Base allowance</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {startup.map((row) => (
                <tr><td>{row.item}</td><td class="num">{row.amt}</td></tr>
              ))}
              <tr class="total"><td>Subtotal, renovation/startup path excluding property acquisition</td><td class="num">$1,650,000</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h4>All-in planning stack</h4>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th class="num">Planning figure</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {capitalStack.map((row) => (
                <tr><td>{row.item}</td><td class="num"><strong>{row.amt}</strong></td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The stack reconciles directly: a $0.7–1.1M acquisition + $1.35M renovation and
          startup allowance + $300K formal working capital + $150–200K additional accessible
          liquidity = approximately <strong>$2.5–2.95M all-in</strong>. A property that cannot
          support this arithmetic does not fit the base case.
        </p>
        <p>
          The preferred strategy shifts from acquire land → design → build to
          <strong>identify an existing property → selectively renovate → establish Leaf &amp;
          Lantern within an existing place</strong>. Adaptive reuse is an
          <strong>investment gate</strong>: a site requiring total investment substantially above
          ≈$2.5–3.0M all-in should receive much greater scrutiny. Peak autumn parking should not
          automatically determine expensive permanently improved lots — gravel overflow,
          reinforced turf, shared parking, and genuine express/preorder lanes are preferred.
        </p>

        <details class="case-study">
          <summary>
            <span class="case-flag">Capital vignette · Optional reading</span>
            <span class="case-title">Why adaptive reuse is the planning base case</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="case-body">
            <p>
              Roughly $2.5–3.0M in project costs excluding land is hazardous given projected
              operating income. New-build with corridor land, full sitework, and escalation
              easily lands near $4.0–4.6M all-in. That capital structure does not produce an
              attractive investor return quickly enough.
            </p>
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="ll-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Preferred property type</th><th class="num">Role</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  {landExamples.map((r) => (
                    <tr><td>{r.parcel}</td><td class="num"><strong>{r.ask}</strong></td><td>{r.note}</td></tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p>
              Ideal characteristics already on site: 4,000–7,000 sq ft usable interior, commercial
              or assembly use, bathrooms, commercial electrical, HVAC, parking, bus/delivery
              access, outdoor gathering space, and proximity to Northville, Novi, South Lyon,
              Plymouth, or the broader western Oakland/Wayne/Livingston market. An established
              property can also contribute known location, community familiarity, architectural
              character, and a narrative that strengthens the brand — modern without requiring a
              newly constructed building.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <h3>Break-even definition</h3>
        <p>
          Cash break-even is the level at which operating cash flow covers all operating
          expenses — including market-rate owner-manager compensation within payroll — plus
          interest and scheduled principal repayment. Under the capital stack (lean labor,
          adaptive-reuse capital, $20–21 ticket, program contribution), cash break-even arrives
          earlier on the ramp — near the <strong>$1.8–2.0M revenue</strong> band —
          while the investment target remains mature <strong>EBITDA of $550–650k+</strong>.
          Financing 65% of a capital-gated ≈$2.75M all-in project at 7% over twenty years
          requires roughly $165K of annual debt service. Income taxes apply only above
          break-even; depreciation is excluded as a non-cash item.
        </p>

        <h3>Investor returns <small>(illustrative path)</small></h3>
        <p>
          Break-even answers an operating question; investor return is a cash-and-exit question.
          Under a capital-gated stack:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Capital structure</th><th class="num">Amount</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {capitalStructure.map((c) => (
                <tr><td>{c.item}</td><td class="num"><strong>{c.amt}</strong></td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div
          class="pb-chart"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Cumulative operating cash — EBITDA minus about 165 thousand dollars of debt service — by year under the management-target case. Year one is approximately cash-flat. Later years compound as mature EBITDA approaches 550 to 650 thousand dollars. This chart does not show equity payback."
        >
          <div class="pb-plot">
            <div class="pb-line pb-zero" style={`bottom: ${pbPct(0)}%;`}><span>$0</span></div>
            <div class="pb-line pb-equity" style={`bottom: ${pbPct(payback.equity)}%;`}><span>{payback.equityLabel}</span></div>
            <div class="pb-cols">
              {payback.years.map((y) => (
                <div class="pb-col">
                  <div
                    class:list={['pb-bar', { 'pb-neg': y.cum < 0 }]}
                    style={
                      y.cum >= 0
                        ? `bottom: ${pbPct(0)}%; height: ${pbPct(y.cum) - pbPct(0)}%;`
                        : `bottom: ${pbPct(y.cum)}%; height: ${pbPct(0) - pbPct(y.cum)}%;`
                    }
                  >
                    <span class="pb-val">{y.cum < 0 ? `−$${Math.abs(y.cum).toLocaleString()}K` : `$${y.cum.toLocaleString()}K`}</span>
                  </div>
                  <span class="pb-year">{y.label}</span>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="figure-note">
          Cumulative operating cash (EBITDA − ≈$165K debt service), pre-tax, under the
          management-target case. Year One is approximately cash-flat; later years compound as
          mature EBITDA approaches $550–650k+. This is an operating-cash chart, not an equity
          payback claim — levered IRR belongs in the investment memorandum.
        </p>
        <p>
          Soft costs — due diligence/design, POS/technology, pre-opening, and working capital —
          remain material and are not dollar-for-dollar recoverable real-estate assets.
          Specialized kitchen and site improvements may also return far less than cost on
          liquidation. <strong>There can be a substantial equity write-off if the operating
          thesis fails</strong>; the downside is not simply a clean property sale. The thesis
          does not depend on aggressive attendance growth or appreciation of a newly
          constructed property.
        </p>
        <p>
          Acquiring an adaptable existing property may preserve more downside value than
          ground-up development by placing a larger share of project capital into an
          established, independently useful real-estate asset. That is a relative advantage,
          not a guarantee of equity recovery.
        </p>
        <p>
          The post-pilot investment memorandum will include what this feasibility plan does not
          yet: a levered IRR, explicit terminal-value assumptions, a replacement-capex reserve,
          and a downside sale scenario. Paths that can still improve investor outcomes after
          pilots clear — continued growth past 85k transactions, a refinance after
          stabilization, acquisition + renovation well inside the $2.5–3.0M envelope, or a
          disciplined exit — remain available, but they are not assumed here. Ownership split,
          distribution policy, and investor preferences are set in that memorandum.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Brand ============ -->
